  "story": {
    "outcome": [
      {
        "text": "56-9-2 in official play; ranked 9 of the pooled top 20 (OPR 107.15, Curie).",
        "basis": "record",
        "src": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "bet": [
      {
        "text": "Positive engagement with the reef: touch it, so every coral goes directly onto a branch.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s13"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "demand": [
      {
        "text": "Reach all four reef levels, score algae directly in the barge and climb the deep cage. The team expected most teams, itself included, to skip a ground intake and litter the floor with coral, so every mechanism had to reach outside the frame.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s9"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "auto": [
      {
        "text": "At the championship the team said its four-piece autos were very consistent, sometimes with a fifth coral in hand. In March its core auto was three L4 coral and one algae; another pushed partners over the line first.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s13",
          "s9"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "cycle": [
      {
        "text": "The human player tosses coral in and the serializer feeds the ejector. The driver picks left or right and a level, the robot locks its angle to that reef section, and a through-beam fires when it meets the reef.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Algae can be pulled as the elevator descends.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s9"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "endgame": [
      {
        "text": "A flap guides the deep cage into place, locks grip it and the side climber rotates down. A ratchet-like stage holds even a partial climb after power is cut.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s13",
          "s9"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "gave_up": [
      {
        "text": "A ground coral intake. Packaging was tight, and ballast in the drivetrain offsets the moving mass.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "A commentator names the lack of a ground intake on its alliance as one of the biggest reasons for a Curie playoff loss, along with penalties while it was blocked in by defenders. It adds that the alliance would still have lost without them.",
        "basis": "observer",
        "src": [
          "s14"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "changed": [
      {
        "text": "In week one the robot tipped easily at L4, so the laterator and ejector were redesigned around a passive cam. Coral jams led to a new powered serializer just before championship. A servo climb lock that cost a couple of climbs became a ratchet-like stage.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The laterator loosened left to right over time and was shimmed for an off-season event.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s15"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "functions": [
    {
      "name": "Drivetrain",
      "does": [
        "drive"
      ],
      "type": "swerve with printed wheels",
      "serves": [],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Frame side, square",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ],
          "value": 29,
          "unit": "in"
        },
        {
          "text": "REV MAXSwerve with custom 3D-printed TPU wheels",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Ballast in the drivetrain offsets the moving mass above",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A 0.25 in gap between frame and bumpers makes the bumpers easy to fit; spacers, missing from the CAD, fill it at less than 8 in intervals",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s18"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Elevator",
      "pieces": [
        "coral",
        "algae"
      ],
      "does": [
        "position"
      ],
      "type": "three-stage elevator",
      "serves": [
        "reef-branches",
        "net"
      ],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "All major subsystems sit inside it",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "It is set toward the back of the robot for a clean coral path",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The elevator uses chain mostly because the team knew it",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s16"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Bearing-to-gusset hard stops began as Markforged Onyx and moved to MaxComposite to save weight; maybe one Onyx stop was replaced, after deforming over time",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s17"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Laterator",
      "pieces": [
        "coral",
        "algae"
      ],
      "solves": [
        {
          "text": "Take and score coral without regard to coral on the floor, which the team says saves seconds per cycle",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "position"
      ],
      "type": "lateral elevator nested in the third elevator stage",
      "serves": [
        "reef-branches"
      ],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Carries the coral serializer, the coral ejector and the algae gripper outside the frame",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A passive cam with printed parts sprung by surgical tubing gives separate intake and score positions as the ejector stows",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cost": [
        {
          "text": "Scoring L4 made the robot very tippy in week one",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "It generally derailed when the robot climbed onto the reef, which it was not designed for, and it loosened left to right over time",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s15"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Coral serializer",
      "pieces": [
        "coral"
      ],
      "solves": [
        {
          "text": "Turn coral tossed in from above into one orientation without sticking",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13",
            "s9"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "acquire",
        "transfer"
      ],
      "type": "chute with powered molded flaps",
      "serves": [
        "reef-branches"
      ],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Custom-molded polyurethane flaps all spin on one Vortex, so coral in any position hits a roller",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The team says the new version saves, per cycle, about",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ],
          "value": 1,
          "unit": "s"
        },
        {
          "text": "It keeps the chute's slope so the human player can toss from odd angles under defense",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cost": [
        {
          "text": "The earlier version, polycarbonate parts and one belt, left coral stuck unless the driver rocked the robot",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Coral ejector",
      "pieces": [
        "coral"
      ],
      "solves": [
        {
          "text": "Know the coral will land on the branch, and fire without waiting to check alignment",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "deliver",
        "retain",
        "position"
      ],
      "type": "sloped-roller ejector with mechanical guides",
      "serves": [
        "reef-branches"
      ],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Four rollers, all sloped to orient the coral",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The inner through-beam detects the coral; the outer one retracts coral that is too far out and fires when it meets the reef",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "SRPP flaps close the last gap mechanically, so the robot need not be perfectly aligned",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9",
            "s13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Its structure is mostly carbon fiber tube and MaxComposite, quite strong in tension",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s15"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cost": [
        {
          "text": "Roller positions were changed to smooth a path that could jam",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Algae gripper",
      "pieces": [
        "algae"
      ],
      "solves": [
        {
          "text": "Take algae from the reef and the floor with no wasted movement",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "acquire",
        "retain",
        "deliver"
      ],
      "type": "two pivoting arms with belt-driven rollers",
      "serves": [
        "processor",
        "net"
      ],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "The arms pivot open to fit and compress the algae",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "It pivots on the laterator base, so it can pull an algae as the elevator comes down from a coral",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cost": [
        {
          "text": "A 15 lb gas shock did not hold algae in build testing; the team's failure sheet recommended 30 to 45 lb. In March the team tested 3D prints in place of gas shocks on its practice robot",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s6",
            "s9"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Climber",
      "solves": [
        {
          "text": "Latch the cage quickly and stay up even if time runs out partway",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "endgame"
      ],
      "type": "side-mounted rotating climber",
      "serves": [
        "cage"
      ],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Rods hook inside the cage and rotate down, pulling the robot up and steadying the chain",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A guide flap pushes the cage into the position needed",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "An added stage locks like a drill chuck as it spins, similar to a ratchet",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cost": [
        {
          "text": "In February the robot climbed twice but began to lower once disabled, so a lock was added",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Vision and reef targeting",
      "does": [
        "sense"
      ],
      "type": "single-camera vision",
      "serves": [
        "reef-branches"
      ],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "One front camera through PhotonVision",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The reef is divided like a pie chart; in a section the robot targets only its two branches and locks to that angle",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "One driver picks the side and the level",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s13"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Carpet characterization",
      "solves": [
        {
          "text": "Wheels travel a different distance with and against the carpet nap; the team says an out-and-back auto run can be off by 6 in or more",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "sense"
      ],
      "type": "direction-dependent odometry correction",
      "serves": [],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Travel was measured with a tape against the robot's own reading in 8 directions relative to the carpet nap, with linear interpolation between them",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The correction uses each module's field position and angle",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "MaxComposite parts",
      "does": [
        "structure"
      ],
      "type": "self-reinforced polypropylene",
      "serves": [],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Used where parts need high strength at low weight",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Cut by laser if possible; after a router, the edges must be melted to seal the woven layers",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s16"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cost": [
        {
          "text": "It delaminates under edge loading",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s16"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "choice": "Contact with the reef",
      "because": "it assures the coral lands and lets the robot fire by itself",
      "instead_of": "firing coral at the branches, which looked inaccurate on early robots",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Elevator at the back of the robot",
      "because": "it gives the coral a clean path and makes a laterator worthwhile",
      "instead_of": "an elevator toward the front",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Mechanical guides on the ejector",
      "because": "the team thought vision would not be accurate enough to get within about 2 in",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Two reef targets per section",
      "because": "it limits complexity; the driver only picks left or right",
      "instead_of": "choosing among all 12",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Ratchet-like climb lock",
      "because": "it locks as it climbs and holds a partial climb",
      "instead_of": "a servo latch that did not act in time in a couple of matches",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "MaxComposite elevator hard stops",
      "because": "they weigh less",
      "instead_of": "Markforged Onyx",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s17"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "A printed PLA sprocket",
      "because": "the joint carries little torque and the sprocket did not fail; the team says walls must be thick enough to make the teeth solid",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s16"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Block CAD before detail CAD",
      "because": "the team says the method worked perfectly for it; block CAD is usually meant to be 90 percent done within a week to a week and a half of kickoff, and the first full robot assembly took about 2.5 to 3 weeks, with revisions up to the championship",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s15",
        "s19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Block CAD mostly from gut feel, then iteration",
      "because": "the team has generally not done much prototyping, and 2025 was no exception",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Lay out wiring in CAD early",
      "because": "a conference abstract quoted in the thread says it streamlined the build and improved reliability and maintainability in 2025",
      "basis": "observer",
      "src": [
        "s20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "Log failures in chat, then in a failure sheet",
      "because": "details fade quickly; every chat message about a failure is tagged so it can be found and moved to the sheet later",
      "cost": "software failures were mostly left out, because that group had its own process",
      "basis": "team",
      "src": [
        "s21"
      ]
    }
  ],
